/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Across the East and Horn of Africa, pastoralist women are already leading climate adaptation and not waiting to be included in it. Evidence from key informant interviews with 20 organizations working in Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa shows that women possess and apply detailed indigenous ecological knowledge of weather patterns, water sources, livestock health, seed preservation and rangeland conditions, and are the primary architects of the everyday coping and adaptation strategies that keep pastoral households functioning through drought, flood and displacement. Yet this leadership is almost entirely informal, undocumented, and unfunded. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Global evidence indicates that only a fraction of one percent of international climate finance reaches initiatives led by Indigenous women \(UN Women, \(as cited in Coelho, 2025\), and this study's evidence suggests pastoralist women in the Horn of Africa face a comparable funding gap. This brief argues that recognizing and resourcing women's existing adaptation leadership through direct finance, formal knowledge integration, and institutional partnership is a more effective and more just starting point for climate resilience programming than treating pastoralist women primarily as beneficiaries of externally designed interventions.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
