/P 104 0 R
/T (Adaptation policy in the Horn of Africa has tended to treat pastoralist women as a vulnerable population to be protected, rather than as practitioners with decades of applied ecological expertise. Such framing bears real costs because it channels resources toward externally designed interventions rather than strengthening what communities and women specifically are already doing. Hence, this leaves women's knowledge systems undocumented and vulnerable to loss as environmental and social conditions change faster than knowledge can be transmitted between and across generations.)
/E (Adaptation policy in the Horn of Africa has tended to treat pastoralist women as a vulnerable population to be protected, rather than as practitioners with decades of applied ecological expertise. Such framing bears real costs because it channels resources toward externally designed interventions rather than strengthening what communities and women specifically are already doing. Hence, this leaves women's knowledge systems undocumented and vulnerable to loss as environmental and social conditions change faster than knowledge can be transmitted between and across generations.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 104 0 R
/T (Our evidence points to this. Accordingly, this brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study through key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania, and South Africa. Across every country in this study, women were identified by respondents as the primary holders of practical climate knowledge, knowledge that is granular, hyper-local and continuously updated through daily interaction with livestock, water sources and rangeland, in contrast to the more generalized, top-down knowledge embedded in many formal early-warning and adaptation systems.)
/E (Our evidence points to this. Accordingly, this brief builds on evidence generated specifically for this study through key informant interviews with 20 pastoralist women-led, humanitarian, climate-resilience, policy and peacebuilding organizations across Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania, and South Africa. Across every country in this study, women were identified by respondents as the primary holders of practical climate knowledge, knowledge that is granular, hyper-local and continuously updated through daily interaction with livestock, water sources and rangeland, in contrast to the more generalized, top-down knowledge embedded in many formal early-warning and adaptation systems.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
