223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (A growing body of literature confirms that women's leadership in climate adaptation is a well-documented global and regional phenomenon, not an emerging or speculative claim. The World Bank \(2022\) documents Indigenous women across multiple regions innovating community-level adaptation and mitigation practices, reviving indigenous seed varieties, establishing seed banks, developing water-harvesting systems and preserving food, a scenario that describes the generational transfer of traditional climate knowledge as having made Indigenous women "climate experts, innovators, and problem-solvers" \(World Bank, 2022\). Evidence from Baringo County, Kenya, provides some of the most granular confirmation available for the East Africa context. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (For example, a 2026 study of pastoral women in Ribkwo, Tiaty Sub-County, found women to be near-universal managers of domestic water and small livestock, and documented emerging hybrid adaptation practices that the authors term "ritual-technological hybridity", whereby women combining indigenous environmental sensing \(such as traditional weather and pasture indicators\) with new digital and mobile-based information sharing, including informal networks of women rain-scouts \(Liru, 2026\). The same study found that while household-level improvisations such as rotational water fetching and dew harvesting provide immediate relief, recurrent coping erodes assets and time in ways that undermine long-term adaptive capacity, directly reinforcing this brief's core argument that unsupported adaptation has limits.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Marty et al. \(2022\) reached a related conclusion in their intersectional analysis of Maasai pastoralists in southern Kenya, finding that households' ability to benefit from livelihood diversification as a climate adaptation strategy is shaped by intersecting inequalities, meaning that not all women, and not all adaptation strategies, have equal chances of success without external support to address the underlying structural constraints. At the continental level, UNDP's Regional Service Centre for Africa found that women are crucial custodians of indigenous knowledge passed down through generations and are frequently the first to notice declining resource quality such as water, soil, and pasture, thus enabling them to lead the search for community-level solutions \(UNDP, 2024\). )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
