/T (UNDP's blog series on women and climate adaptation in Africa similarly documents that in Kenya, women produce up to eighty percent of the country's food, yet continue to struggle to secure financing, climate-smart technology and land titles necessary to convert their labor and knowledge into durable resilience \(UNDP, 2025\).)
/E (UNDP's blog series on women and climate adaptation in Africa similarly documents that in Kenya, women produce up to eighty percent of the country's food, yet continue to struggle to secure financing, climate-smart technology and land titles necessary to convert their labor and knowledge into durable resilience \(UNDP, 2025\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 107 0 R
/T (The finance gap identified in this brief is corroborated by global monitoring data. UN Women's tracking of international climate finance flows found that only an estimated 0.01 percent of global climate finance reaches initiatives led directly by Indigenous women \(Coelho, 2025\), Crucially, this figure helps explain why the adaptation strategies documented in this study remain informal, small-scale and self-financed despite their demonstrated effectiveness and reach. The World Resources Institute's review of locally led climate adaptation mechanisms similarly finds that intermediated funding structures, where finance must pass through multiple institutional layers before reaching community organizations remain one of the most significant barriers to scaling women-led and community-led adaptation across Africa \(World Resources Institute, 2025\).)
/E (The finance gap identified in this brief is corroborated by global monitoring data. UN Women's tracking of international climate finance flows found that only an estimated 0.01 percent of global climate finance reaches initiatives led directly by Indigenous women \(Coelho, 2025\), Crucially, this figure helps explain why the adaptation strategies documented in this study remain informal, small-scale and self-financed despite their demonstrated effectiveness and reach. The World Resources Institute's review of locally led climate adaptation mechanisms similarly finds that intermediated funding structures, where finance must pass through multiple institutional layers before reaching community organizations remain one of the most significant barriers to scaling women-led and community-led adaptation across Africa \(World Resources Institute, 2025\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 107 0 R
/T (Comparative evidence from outside the Horn of Africa situates these findings within a wider global pattern. Research on Indigenous women's climate leadership in Peru documents that women's leadership development is frequently constrained not by lack of capacity but by limited institutional recognition, inadequate integration of Indigenous knowledge into formal governance, and barriers to women's participation in environmental decision-making forums \(Flores et al, 2024\). Research from Indigenous communities in Western Canada similarly repositions Indigenous women as knowledge-keepers and agents of change rather than passive subjects of climate vulnerability, arguing that centering women's land-based knowledge and leadership, rather than treating it as supplementary to scientific or technical adaptation approaches is essential to durable climate resilience \(Datta et al, 2024\).)
/E (Comparative evidence from outside the Horn of Africa situates these findings within a wider global pattern. Research on Indigenous women's climate leadership in Peru documents that women's leadership development is frequently constrained not by lack of capacity but by limited institutional recognition, inadequate integration of Indigenous knowledge into formal governance, and barriers to women's participation in environmental decision-making forums \(Flores et al, 2024\). Research from Indigenous communities in Western Canada similarly repositions Indigenous women as knowledge-keepers and agents of change rather than passive subjects of climate vulnerability, arguing that centering women's land-based knowledge and leadership, rather than treating it as supplementary to scientific or technical adaptation approaches is essential to durable climate resilience \(Datta et al, 2024\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
