253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Women are not adapting in theory; they are adapting daily, through petty trade, milk sales, beadwork, poultry keeping, savings groups, food preservation, seed saving and water conservation. These strategies were described across the dataset as continuous, practical responses to shifting conditions as opposed to once-off interventions, but an ongoing management practice. Respondents were clear, however, that these strategies operate almost entirely without external technical or financial support, leaving women to absorb the full cost of experimentation, failure and adjustment themselves.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (Marty et al. \(2023\) found a comparable pattern among Maasai pastoralists transitioning to diversified livelihoods in southern Kenya, noting that households' ability to succeed with diversification as an adaptation strategy depends heavily on pre-existing access to assets, information and social networks suggesting that unsupported adaptation systematically favours those already better positioned, rather than being equally accessible to all women.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 109 0 R
/T (3. Recurrent shocks are testing the limits of unsupported resilience)
/E (3. Recurrent shocks are testing the limits of unsupported resilience)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (While women's adaptation strategies demonstrate real innovation and agency, respondents across the dataset cautioned that repeated, closely spaced shocks are eroding the assets, time and social capital that these strategies depend on. Coping measures that once represented adaptive resilience such as reducing meals, drawing down savings, informal borrowing are increasingly indistinguishable from emergency survival.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (This mirrors the Baringo County findings directly: household-level improvisations such as rotational water fetching and dew harvesting provide only fragile, short-term relief, and recurrent coping erodes the very assets and time that longer-term adaptive capacity requires \(Liru, 2026\). The same study's introduction of "ritual-technological hybridity" , referring to women blending indigenous sensing practices with mobile-based information sharing illustrates both the ingenuity of women's adaptation and its continued fragility without institutional backing.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
