/T (4. Indigenous knowledge remains excluded from formal systems)
/E (4. Indigenous knowledge remains excluded from formal systems)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (Despite its demonstrated value, women's ecological knowledge remains largely absent from formal early-warning systems, agricultural extension services, climate research and school curricula. Respondents described a persistent institutional tendency to treat indigenous knowledge as anecdotal or outdated, rather than validating and integrating it alongside scientific and technical climate information. Significantly, this gap is well documented at a global level. A systematic review of the peer-reviewed literature on nomadic pastoralism and climate adaptation found that while international frameworks such as the UN's Local Communities and Indigenous Peoples Platform aim to give Indigenous knowledge a more active role in shaping climate action, substantial barriers remain, rooted largely in the comparatively lower institutional power of local and Indigenous knowledge-holders relative to formal scientific and governmental actors \(Tugjamba et al, 2023\).)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 110 0 R
/T (5. Locally led adaptation is effective, but structurally under-financed)
/E (5. Locally led adaptation is effective, but structurally under-financed)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
