275 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 111 0 R
/T (The evidence in this brief supports a reframing of pastoralist women's relationship to climate adaptation. Rather than positioning women as recipients of adaptation assistance, the evidence shows women already functioning as adaptation leaders and they apply detailed ecological knowledge, sustaining functioning livelihoods through recurrent shocks, and innovating new hybrid practices that blend indigenous and modern information sources. )
/E (The evidence in this brief supports a reframing of pastoralist women's relationship to climate adaptation. Rather than positioning women as recipients of adaptation assistance, the evidence shows women already functioning as adaptation leaders and they apply detailed ecological knowledge, sustaining functioning livelihoods through recurrent shocks, and innovating new hybrid practices that blend indigenous and modern information sources. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 111 0 R
/T (The binding constraint on scaling this leadership is not capacity, knowledge or motivation. It is the near-total absence of direct finance, formal recognition and institutional partnership. Programming that continues to design adaptation interventions around pastoralist women, rather than resourcing and building on what women are already doing and already know, will continue to under-perform relative to what locally led, women-driven adaptation has already demonstrated it can achieve.)
/E (The binding constraint on scaling this leadership is not capacity, knowledge or motivation. It is the near-total absence of direct finance, formal recognition and institutional partnership. Programming that continues to design adaptation interventions around pastoralist women, rather than resourcing and building on what women are already doing and already know, will continue to under-perform relative to what locally led, women-driven adaptation has already demonstrated it can achieve.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
