296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 113 0 R
/T (3. Indigenous Knowledge Integration)
/E (3. Indigenous Knowledge Integration)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 624 0 R
/E (Document and validate indigenous ecological knowledge - support participatory, women-led documentation of local weather indicators, water and pasture knowledge, seed varieties and livestock health practices, with appropriate intellectual and cultural protections for knowledge holders.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 625 0 R
/E (Integrate indigenous knowledge into formal early-warning systems directly - co-design early-warning triggers with women knowledge-holders using models such as IGAD's Forecasts to Local Action \(FOLA\) approach, which builds anticipatory-action triggers together with pastoralist communities, including documented sessions with women in Karamoja rather than broadcasting forecasts top-down. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 626 0 R
/E (Support hybrid knowledge innovation - invest in the kind of practical, women-led innovation already emerging organically such as mobile-based information-sharing networks that complement indigenous environmental sensing rather than importing externally designed digital tools devoid of community input.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 113 0 R
/T (4. Institutional Partnership, Not Parallel Structures)
/E (4. Institutional Partnership, Not Parallel Structures)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 627 0 R
/E (Strengthen and not replace existing women's networks - channel technical and financial support through savings groups, cooperatives and informal solidarity networks that already have community trust and local reach, rather than creating parallel structures.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 628 0 R
/E (Build an intergenerational knowledge-transmission strategy - invest in structured mechanisms for transmitting indigenous ecological knowledge to younger women, given evidence that reliance on informal generational transfer alone is vulnerable to disruption as environmental and social conditions change.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
