1230 0 obj
<<
/Title (Adaptive capacity is not the constraint - asset ownership and formal authority are. Women adapt despite structural exclusion, not because of institutional backing.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1219 0 R
/Prev 1225 0 R
/Next 1231 0 R
>>
endobj
1231 0 obj
<<
/Title (Recurrent shocks are testing the limits of unsupported adaptation; without investment, informal strategies risk collapsing into distress coping.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1219 0 R
/Prev 1230 0 R
/Next 1226 0 R
>>
endobj
1232 0 obj
<<
/Title (The finance gap identified in this brief is corroborated by global monitoring data. UN Women's tracking of international climate finance flows found that only an estimated 0.01 percent of global climate finance reaches initiatives led directly by Indigenous women \(Coelho, 2025\), Crucially, this figure helps explain why the adaptation strategies documented in this study remain informal, small-scale and self-financed despite their demonstrated effectiveness and reach. The World Resources Institute's review of locally led climate adaptation mechanisms similarly finds that intermediated funding structures, where finance must pass through multiple institutional layers before reaching community organizations remain one of the most significant barriers to scaling women-led and community-led adaptation across Africa \(World Resources Institute, 2025\).)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1227 0 R
/Next 1233 0 R
>>
endobj
1233 0 obj
<<
/Title (Comparative evidence from outside the Horn of Africa situates these findings within a wider global pattern. Research on Indigenous women's climate leadership in Peru documents that women's leadership development is frequently constrained not by lack of capacity but by limited institutional recognition, inadequate integration of Indigenous knowledge into formal governance, and barriers to women's participation in environmental decision-making forums \(Flores et al, 2024\). Research from Indigenous communities in Western Canada similarly repositions Indigenous women as knowledge-keepers and agents of change rather than passive subjects of climate vulnerability, arguing that centering women's land-based knowledge and leadership, rather than treating it as supplementary to scientific or technical adaptation approaches is essential to durable climate resilience \(Datta et al, 2024\).)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1232 0 R
/Next 1228 0 R
>>
endobj
1234 0 obj
<<
/Title (Across all four study countries notably Kenya, Somalia, Tanzania and South Africa, key informants consistently identified women as the principal holders and transmitters of practical ecological knowledge, including reading weather and seasonal patterns, tracking water source reliability, identifying edible and medicinal plants, managing seed varieties, and recognizing early signs of livestock illness or pasture degradation. This knowledge is typically acquired through direct, sustained daily engagement with the environment over decades, and is transmitted informally from older to younger women rather than through any formal institution.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1229 0 R
/Next 1236 0 R
>>
endobj
1235 0 obj
<<
/Title (This finding is well supported in the wider literature. The World Bank \(2022\) documents Indigenous women's climate knowledge as the product of generational transfer that has made women climate experts, innovators, and problem-solvers in their communities, while research from Baringo County \(Liru, 2026\) documents specific hybrid practices which include informal women-led weather-scouting networks that combine this indigenous knowledge base with newer digital tools.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1229 0 R
/Prev 1236 0 R
>>
endobj
